{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.lsus.edu/Documents/HR/Job%20Opportunities/2025%20LSUS%20Assistant%20Professor%20of%20Health%20Administration.docx": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Heath Administration",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Heath Administration. Assistant Professor of Health Administration\nThe AACSB accredited College of Business at Louisiana State University Shreveport (LSUS) invites applications for two 9-month tenure-track positions in Health Administration at the rank of Assistant Professor starting August 2026. The selected candidate will report to the Chair of the James K. Elrod Department of Health Administration. Applicants will be considered from Health Administration or related fields for individuals who meet our AACSB qualifications.\nResponsibilities:\nTeach Health Administration courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels, in face-to-face and online settings.\nProduce scholarship at a level consistent with our AACSB Scholarly Academic standards.\nParticipate in service activities at the university, college, and department levels, as well as in community and profession.\nParticipate in the assessment of student learning outcomes and program effectiveness.\nRequired Qualifications:\nA doctoral degree in Health Administration or a related discipline with a strong research background in business from an AACSB accredited university. ABD applicants with an expected completion date prior to August 2026 will be considered.\nEvidence of effective teaching and research commensurate with years of experience.\nPreferred Qualifications:\nAt least one year of demonstrated teaching effectiveness, teaching Health Administration classes.\nPosition-related certifications.\nSalary and Benefits:\nCompetitive salary commensurate with experience.\nComprehensive benefits package, including health insurance, retirement plan options, and professional development support.\nApplication Process:\nTo apply for this position, a CV, cover letter, statement of teaching philosophy, copies of all transcripts that include relevant course work, and contact details of three references should be sent electronically to businessfacultysearch@lsus.edu. Review of complete applications will begin on November 1, 2025. The search will remain open until the position is filled. Selected candidates for the interview will be asked to provide three letters of recommendation. LSUS is an Affirmative Action and Equal Opportunity Employer. To be considered, the email subject must be “Tenure Track Health Administration Faculty Application.”\nAbout Louisiana State University–Shreveport College of Business:\nNational Recognition: Established in 1967, Louisiana State University–Shreveport is a public university known for academic excellence and affordability.\nCollege of Business:\nOne of the largest business colleges in the region.\nOver 6,500 students enrolled in the Master of Business Administration (MBA) and Master of Health Administration (MHA) programs.\nNearly 1,000 undergraduate business students enrolled in majors across accounting, finance, general business, management, and marketing.\nFaculty represents five continents and more than 18 countries.\nMission-Driven: Grounded in its AACSB-aligned mission to educate, engage, and empower students.\nWorking Professional-Centric Graduate Programs: Offers 100% accelerated online MBA and MHA programs.\nFlexible Learning: Offers both accelerated online and traditional face-to-face undergraduate programs.\nImpact-Focused Education: Prepares students to make business decisions that create positive and lasting societal impact.\nAbout Shreveport-Bossier, LA:\nWhy Consider Living in the Shreveport–Bossier Area\nRegional Hub: Educational, medical, and cultural center of North Louisiana.\nPopulation & Size: Metropolitan area of more than 390,000 residents, offering mid-sized city amenities with a manageable pace of life.\nAffordable Living: Low cost of living and housing well below national averages, allowing professionals and families to enjoy a higher standard of living.\nEasy Travel: Shreveport Regional Airport provides convenient connections to major hubs including Dallas, Houston, and Atlanta—ideal for conferences and research travel.\nCultural Life: Symphony performances, film festivals, art galleries, live music venues, and an array of dining options including Louisiana Creole and Cajun traditions enrich the community.\nOutdoor Recreation: Nearby rivers, lakes, and wildlife areas offer hiking, kayaking, cycling, and other outdoor activities.\nBalanced Lifestyle: Combines affordability, accessibility, cultural amenities, and natural spaces—making it an appealing place to live, work, and grow."
    },
    "https://www.lsus.edu/Documents/HR/Job%20Opportunities/Cloyd%20Professor%20of%20Real%20Estate.docx": {
      "title": "Associate/Full Professor of Real Estate",
      "description": "Associate/Full Professor of Real Estate. Associate or Full Professor of Real Estate\nThe AACSB accredited College of Business at Louisiana State University Shreveport (LSUS) invites applications for the Oscar Cloyd Real Estate Super Professorship, a 9-month tenure-track position in Real Estate at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor starting August 2026. The selected candidate will report to the Chair of the Department of Economics and Finance. Applicants from the area of Real Estate or Finance, with appropriate qualifications to teach real estate, will be considered for individuals who meet our AACSB qualifications.\nResponsibilities:\nTeach Real Estate and Finance courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels, in face-to-face and online settings.\nProduce scholarship at a level consistent with our AACSB Scholarly Academic standards.\nParticipate in service activities at the university, college, and department levels, as well as in community and profession.\nParticipate in the assessment of student learning outcomes and program effectiveness.\nAdditional duties, as required by the original endowment, include:\ncontinue a record of scholarly and creative endeavors;\nexhibit leadership in academic units, collaborations with other scholars, and the private sector;\npursue activities that enhance the reputation of the university;\nperform exceptionally in the areas of innovative teaching, student advisement, mentoring, and leadership in curriculum development.\nRequired Qualifications:\nA doctoral degree in Real Estate, or Finance with a specialty in Real Estate, or related degree with appropriate qualifications to teach real estate, with a strong research background in business from an AACSB accredited university.\nEvidence of effective teaching and research commensurate with years of experience.\nPreferred Qualifications:\nA demonstrated record of teaching effectiveness in multiple areas of real estate and finance classes, such as Real Estate Principles, Real Estate Appraisal, and Real Estate Investments.\nPosition-related certifications.\nSalary and Benefits:\nThe Oscar Cloyd Super Professorship carries a generous stipend for travel and research on top of the base salary.\nCompetitive salary commensurate with experience\nComprehensive benefits package, including health insurance, retirement plan options, and professional development support\nApplication Process:\nTo apply for this position, a CV, cover letter, statement of teaching philosophy, copies of all transcripts that include relevant course work, and contact details of three references should be sent electronically to businessfacultysearch@lsus.edu. Review of complete applications will begin on December 1, 2025. The search will remain open until the position is filled. Selected candidates for the interview will be asked to provide three letters of recommendation. LSUS is an Affirmative Action and Equal Opportunity Employer. To be considered, the email subject must be “Tenure Track Real Estate Faculty Application.”\nAbout Louisiana State University–Shreveport College of Business:\nNational Recognition: Established in 1967, Louisiana State University–Shreveport is a public university known for academic excellence and affordability.\nCollege of Business:\nOne of the largest business colleges in the region.\nOver 6,500 students enrolled in the Master of Business Administration (MBA) and Master of Health Administration (MHA) programs.\nNearly 1,000 undergraduate business students enrolled in majors across accounting, finance, general business, management, and marketing.\nFaculty represents five continents and more than 18 countries.\nMission-Driven: Grounded in its AACSB-aligned mission to educate, engage, and empower students.\nWorking Professional-Centric Graduate Programs: Offers 100% accelerated online MBA and MHA programs.\nFlexible Learning: Offers both accelerated online and traditional face-to-face undergraduate programs.\nImpact-Focused Education: Prepares students to make business decisions that create positive and lasting societal impact.\nAbout Shreveport-Bossier, LA:\nWhy Consider Living in the Shreveport–Bossier Area\nRegional Hub: Educational, medical, and cultural center of North Louisiana.\nPopulation & Size: Metropolitan area of more than 390,000 residents, offering mid-sized city amenities with a manageable pace of life.\nAffordable Living: Low cost of living and housing well below national averages, allowing professionals and families to enjoy a higher standard of living.\nEasy Travel: Shreveport Regional Airport provides convenient connections to major hubs including Dallas, Houston, and Atlanta—ideal for conferences and research travel.\nCultural Life: Symphony performances, film festivals, art galleries, live music venues, and an array of dining options including Louisiana Creole and Cajun traditions enrich the community.\nOutdoor Recreation: Nearby rivers, lakes, and wildlife areas offer hiking, kayaking, cycling, and other outdoor activities.\nBalanced Lifestyle: Combines affordability, accessibility, cultural amenities, and natural spaces—making it an appealing place to live, work, and grow."
    },
    "https://www.lsus.edu/Documents/HR/Job%20Opportunities/Director%20of%20Access%20Discovery%20July%202026%20Job%20Description.docx": {
      "title": "Director of Access & Discovery",
      "description": "Director of Access & Discovery. Director of Access and Discovery\n12 month, Tenure – Track Position / Assistant Librarian\nThe LSUS Noel Memorial Library seeks a user-focused, outcomes-based, collaborative professional for the position of Director of Access and Discovery, a full-time, 12-month tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Librarian, reporting to the Dean of the Noel Memorial Library. The director will provide strategic leadership over technical services such as collection strategies, discovery and metadata services, library technologies, resource sharing, scholarly communications, and user experience. The director will develop and implement divisional goals, priorities, and assessment measures that align with the library and university strategic plans. The director will supervise, mentor, and evaluate faculty, staff, and student employees within the division, and additionally serve on behalf of the Dean, when designated.\nDuties and Responsibilities\nCollection Strategies and Resource Sharing\nLead collection strategy initiatives and acquisition operations that support institutional curricula, research, and community engagement. Develop sustainable collection models informed by usage data, curricular needs, and budget priorities. Collaborate with special collections and digital scholarship personnel to expand digital access to unique collections. Provide leadership and oversight for document delivery and resource sharing services. Use assessment data and service analytics to improve workflows, expand resource-sharing partnerships, and enhance user satisfaction.\nDiscovery Services and Library Systems\nProvide leadership for library content and discovery platforms, metadata creation, cataloging operations, and supporting infrastructure. Develop initiatives that enhance discoverability, accessibility, and integration of resources. Serve as the library’s liaison with campus ITS and the LOUIS consortium systems administrator. Coordinate metadata practices across physical collections, electronic resources, digital collections, and institutional repositories. Serve as original cataloger. Monitor emerging technologies, including AI-enhanced discovery and service tools, and recommend adoption strategies consistent with institutional goals. Ensure reliable, secure, and accessible access to student technology, library systems, and digital resources.\nOpen Education and Scholarship\nChampion a user-centered approach to promote open scholarship, open educational resources, and research dissemination initiatives. Monitor trends in scholarly publishing, open access, and transformative agreements. Oversee the efforts of the Scholarly Communications Librarian.\nData Assessment and User Experience\nDevelop assessment strategies that measure the effectiveness of electronic resources and discovery services. Recommend emerging technologies, enhancements, and user-centered design trends that improve\noperational efficiency and user engagement. Create consistent assessment and feedback loops for review and evaluation to sustain and improve services and workflows. Oversee Affordable Learning efforts and reporting requirements. Lead usability studies and continuous improvement initiatives that enhance resource discovery and user success.\nCampus and Professional Engagement\nConsult with library leadership in developing a plan for professional development, scholarship, and service relevant to the library and university’s strategic plan. Demonstrate sustained efforts of contribution to the library, university, and professional community. Build strong partnerships with academic departments, faculty, student services, information technology, and regional library organizations. Advocate for library services and resources through targeted outreach and training.\nRequired Qualifications\nMaster’s degree from an ALA accredited library or information science OR;\nan advanced degree beyond the bachelor’s from a regionally accredited college or university plus varied experience in college and university libraries\nExperience working with electronic resources in an academic library\nExperience with COUNTER statistics, SUSHI harvesting, and assessment methods.\nExperience with metadata description protocols and standards (e.g., Dublin Core, DACS, MARC)\nAbility to meet library tenure requirements by established university deadlines\nPreferred Qualifications\nExperience with Ex Libris’ Alma and Primo library service platforms\nExperience managing digital preservation or content management platforms\nExperience with other library-related products (e.g,. EZProxy, ILLiad, LibGuides);\nSalary: Commensurate with experience or $60,000\nDeadline: Open until filled. Applications received by August 14th will have full consideration.\nHow to apply: Email a cover letter, CV, and contact information for at least three professional references with the subject line - Attn: Director of Access and Discovery Application to:\nKarley Leone, Assistant to the Dean\nLSU Shreveport - Noel Memorial Library\nlibrary@LSUS.edu\nLSUS is an Affirmative Action and Equal Opportunity Employer."
    },
    "https://www.lsus.edu/Documents/HR/Job%20Opportunities/2026%20Assistant%20Professor%20of%20Plant%20Biology.docx": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Plant Biology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Plant Biology. Assistant Professor of Plant Biology\nLSU Shreveport invites applications for the position of Assistant Professor of Plant Biology in the Department of Biological Sciences, to begin August 2027. We seek a colleague committed to excellence in teaching, scholarly engagement, and student success in a diverse and inclusive environment. This position is a full time, tenure-track faculty position reporting to the Chair of the Department of Biological Sciences.\nResponsibilities:\nTeach courses in the undergraduate and graduate curriculum and develop elective courses that complement departmental strengths.\nDevelop and maintain an active, externally funded research program that involves undergraduate and graduate students.\nSupervise and mentor students in their academic and professional development.\nContribute to departmental, college, and university service, including advising and mentoring.\nEngage in community outreach.\nRequired Qualifications:\nPh.D. in Biological Sciences or a closely related field by time of appointment. ABD considered with completion by start date.\nPreferred Qualifications:\nPostdoctoral experience.\nEvidence of High-Impact Teaching Practices.\nDemonstrated record of research productivity.\nSalary and Benefits:\nCompetitive salary commensurate with experience.\nComprehensive benefits package, including health insurance, retirement plan options, and professional development support.\nApplication Process:\nPlease submit a cover letter, CV, statements of teaching and research interests, and contact information for three (3) professional references electronically to Dr. Chris Gissendanner, Chair of the Department of Biological Sciences, LSUS, chris.gissendanner@lsus.edu. Questions about the position can also be sent to this email address.\nFor accessibility accommodation during any part of the hiring process, contact Cindy Maggio 318.797.5071.\nLSUS is an equal opportunity employer.\nReview of applications will begin immediately and continue until position is filled.\nLSUS is an Affirmative Action, Equal Opportunity Employer.\nAbout LSU Shreveport and the Department of Biological Sciences:\nLSUS is a teaching-focused, regional public university with a mission that balances excellence in teaching, active scholarship, and community engagement. Named by the U.S. Department of Education as one of the most affordable colleges in Louisiana, LSU Shreveport offers high-quality classroom instruction with very affordable tuition rates. In addition, LSUS offers many forms of financial aid, including scholarships, on-campus employment, and graduate assistantships. With recent expansion in the university’s infrastructure and investment in educational technology, these improvements continue to enhance the student experience at LSU Shreveport, and the University has proven itself to be an institution of opportunity and quality.\nThe Department of Biological Sciences is collegial and inclusive, with active collaboration among faculty spanning departments. Shared lab space and equipment are housed in the Science Center, which includes a Cell Culture Room and Microscopy Suite, a Geographic Information Systems Lab, and the neighboring Technology Building which is home to the Biotechnology Collaborative Laboratory (equipped with PCR, imaging, flow cytometry, sequencing, and other modern tools). Additional resources include The Museum of Life Sciences and the Science Research Annex, which offers modern laboratory facilities including plant and animal care space. Faculty also benefit from connections to a thriving local network of research and outreach institutions including the LSU AgCenter, the LSU Health Sciences Center–Shreveport (LSUHSC-S), the Red River Watershed Management Institute, the Louisiana Universities Marine Consortium (LUMCON), and the Ouachita Mountains Biological Station (OMBS).\nAbout Shreveport:\nThe Shreveport-Bossier City area offers an attractive quality of life, combining the conveniences of a big city with the warmth and hospitality of a smaller town. With a metropolitan population of more than 380,000, the Shreveport-Bossier City area offers a low cost of living, affordable housing, and many diverse dining and entertainment options. Exceptional outdoor recreation opportunities abound. Frequently called \"A Sportsman's Paradise,\" the area's mild climate, various lakes and rivers, and beautiful parks create the perfect setting for jogging, bicycling, water sports, hunting and fishing. For other recreational activities, Shreveport-Bossier is home to riverboat casinos and horse racing at Louisiana Downs. Additional entertainment venues include the Brookshire Grocery Arena which hosts numerous musical events, comedians, rodeos, children’s events and ice-skating productions, among others. Shreveport also hosts dozens of festivals such as Mardi Gras, Louisiana Prize Fest, the Red River Revel, and Taco Wars, with regional food and music, and offers regular theatrical productions, ballet performances, as well as performances by the Shreveport Symphony and the Shreveport Opera. Shreveport is also home to the American Rose Garden and the R.W. Norton Art Gallery."
    },
    "https://www.lsus.edu/Documents/HR/Job%20Opportunities/2026%20Assistant%20Professor%20of%20Art%20History.docx": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Art History",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Art History. Assistant Professor in Art History\nLSU Shreveport invites applications for a 9-month, tenure-track position as Assistant Professor of Art History, beginning August 2027. We seek a colleague committed to excellence in teaching, scholarly research, and student success in a diverse and inclusive environment.\nThe desired applicant will hold a Ph.D. in Art History or a closely related field by August 18, 2027. All full-time faculty teach 12 credit hours per semester. The successful candidate will teach a range of art history courses, including lower-level survey courses, upper-level specialized topics, and the opportunity to contribute at the graduate level to our interdisciplinary Master of Liberal Arts program. The position reports to the Chair of the Department of Arts and Media.\nResponsibilities:\nTeach lower-level survey courses and upper-level/specialized courses in art history.\nRevise existing art history courses and develop new courses to broaden and strengthen the curriculum.\nMentor students in academic and professional development.\nMaintain an active program of scholarly research and publication.\nParticipate in service at the departmental, college, and university levels.\nRequired Qualifications:\nPh.D. in Art History or closely related field (in hand by August 18, 2027).\nDemonstrated ability to teach introductory and advanced courses in art history.\nEvidence of scholarly research with potential for continued productivity.\nDemonstrated commitment to teaching excellence and commitment to accessible teaching practices.\nPreferred Qualifications:\nTeaching experience at the college level.\nAbility to teach courses across a broad chronological and geographical range.\nDemonstrated interest in interdisciplinary collaboration and/or community engagement.\nCommitment to innovative pedagogy and student success.\nSalary and Benefits:\nCompetitive salary commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package, including health insurance, retirement plan options, and professional development support.\nApplication Process:\nDigital application packets are preferred and should be emailed (with “Art History Application” as the subject line) to Faith Phillips, Arts & Media Administrative Assistant, at faith.phillips@lsus.edu.\nReview of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. However, we will give priority to applications received by Friday, October 30th, 2026.\nFor accessibility accommodations during any part of the hiring process, contact disabilityservices@lsus.edu / 318-797-5116.\nLSUS is an Equal Opportunity Employer\nApplication materials must include:\nLetter of application\nCurrent curriculum vitae\nThree current letters of reference\nTeaching statement and sample syllabus\nList of courses taught (including teaching assistant experience, if applicable)\nAbout the Department of Arts and Media:\nThe Department of Arts and Media at LSU Shreveport is a vibrant, interdisciplinary hub that brings together faculty and students from Digital Arts, Mass Communication, Theater, Music, and Communication Studies. The department fosters creativity and collaboration across disciplines, encouraging innovative teaching, research, and performance. Faculty and students regularly engage in projects that cross departmental and college boundaries, reflecting LSUS’s commitment to integrative learning and community engagement. This collaborative spirit makes Arts and Media an exciting place for both academic and creative growth.\nAbout LSU Shreveport and Shreveport:\nLSUS boasts a high percentage of faculty with terminal degrees. With over 10,000 students, LSUS boasts the highest number of graduate students in the state. LSUS has more than 20 undergraduate degree programs, and more than a dozen graduate programs, including a Doctor of Education (Ed.D.) in Leadership Studies. Named by the U.S. Department of Education as one of the most affordable colleges in Louisiana, LSU Shreveport offers high-quality classroom instruction with very affordable tuition rates. In addition, LSUS offers many forms of financial aid, including scholarships, on-campus employment, and graduate assistantships. With recent expansion in the university’s infrastructure and investment in educational technology, these improvements continue to enhance the student experience at LSU Shreveport, and the University has proven itself to be an institution of opportunity and quality.\nThe Shreveport-Bossier City area offers an attractive quality of life, combining the conveniences of a big city with the warmth and hospitality of a smaller town. With a metropolitan population of more than 380,000, the Shreveport-Bossier City area offers a low cost of living, affordable housing, and many diverse dining and entertainment options. Exceptional outdoor recreation opportunities abound. Frequently called 'A Sportsman's Paradise,' the area's mild climate, various lakes and rivers, and beautiful parks create the perfect setting for jogging, bicycling, water sports, hunting and fishing. For other recreational activities, Shreveport-Bossier is home to riverboat casinos and horse racing at Louisiana Downs. Additional entertainment venues include the Brookshire Grocery Arena which hosts numerous musical events, comedians, rodeos, children’s events and ice-skating productions, among others. Shreveport also hosts dozens of festivals such as Mardi Gras, Louisiana Prize Fest, the Red River Revel, and Taco Wars, with regional food and music, and offers regular theatrical productions, ballet performances, as well as performances by the Shreveport Symphony and the Shreveport Opera. Shreveport is also home to the American Rose Garden."
    },
    "https://www.lsus.edu/Documents/HR/Job%20Opportunities/2026%20Assistant%20Professor%20of%20Digital%20Arts.docx": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Digital Arts",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Digital Arts. Assistant Professor in Graphic Design and Digital Arts\nLSU Shreveport invites applications for a tenure-track, 9-month Assistant Professor in Graphic Design and Digital Arts, beginning August 2027. We seek a colleague committed to excellence in teaching, creative scholarship, and student success in a diverse and inclusive environment.\nThe desired applicant will hold an MFA in Graphic Design, Digital Arts, or a related field by August 18, 2027. All full-time faculty teach 12 credit hours per semester across lower-division and upper-level courses. Teaching areas will include graphic design, digital art, and related courses. Candidates must demonstrate comprehensive knowledge of the Adobe Creative Suite.\nResponsibilities:\nTeach undergraduate courses in graphic design, digital art, and related areas.\nMentor students, supporting their academic and creative development.\nEngage in creative/scholarly activity within the discipline.\nParticipate in service at the departmental, college, and university levels.\nContribute to curriculum development and program growth in the Digital Arts Program.\nRequired Qualifications:\nMFA in Graphic Design, Digital Arts, or related field (in hand by August 18, 2027).\nDemonstrated knowledge and proficiency in Adobe Creative Suite.\nDemonstrated commitment to teaching excellence and commitment to accessible teaching practices.\nPreferred Qualifications:\nExperience teaching a range of courses in digital arts and graphic design.\nEvidence of creative or scholarly achievement with potential for continued growth.\nDemonstrated commitment to collaborative projects and community engagement.\nSalary and Benefits:\nCompetitive salary commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package, including health insurance, retirement plan options, and professional development support.\nApplication Process:\nDigital application packets are preferred and should be emailed (with “Digital Arts Application” as the subject line) to Faith Phillips, Arts & Media Administrative Assistant, at faith.phillips@lsus.edu.\nReview of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. However, we will give priority to applications received by Friday, October 30th, 2026.\nFor accessibility accommodations during any part of the hiring process, contact disabilityservices@lsus.edu / 318-797-5116.\nLSUS is an Equal Opportunity Employer\nApplication materials must include:\nLetter of application\nCurrent curriculum vitae\nThree current letters of reference\nLink to a website with 20 images or a demo reel of personal work\nLink to 20 images or videos of student work\nList of courses taught (including teaching assistant experience, if applicable)\nAbout the Digital Arts Program:\nThe BFA in Digital Arts at LSU Shreveport is a dynamic, growing program that prepares students for careers in graphic design, 3D modeling, interactive media, and more. Faculty enjoy teaching motivated students, access to dedicated labs and Adobe Creative Cloud software, and opportunities to engage in collaborative projects, exhibitions, and community partnerships. As part of a collegial department committed to both creative excellence and student success, new faculty will help shape the future of digital arts education in a program recognized for its innovation and impact.\nAbout LSU Shreveport and Shreveport:\nLSUS boasts a high percentage of faculty with terminal degrees. With over 10,000 students, LSUS boasts the highest number of graduate students in the state. LSUS has more than 20 undergraduate degree programs, and more than a dozen graduate programs, including a Doctor of Education (Ed.D.) in Leadership Studies. Named by the U.S. Department of Education as one of the most affordable colleges in Louisiana, LSU Shreveport offers high-quality classroom instruction with very affordable tuition rates. In addition, LSUS offers many forms of financial aid, including scholarships, on-campus employment, and graduate assistantships. With recent expansion in the university’s infrastructure and investment in educational technology, these improvements continue to enhance the student experience at LSU Shreveport, and the University has proven itself to be an institution of opportunity and quality.\nThe Shreveport-Bossier City area offers an attractive quality of life, combining the conveniences of a big city with the warmth and hospitality of a smaller town. With a metropolitan population of more than 380,000, the Shreveport-Bossier City area offers a low cost of living, affordable housing, and many diverse dining and entertainment options. Exceptional outdoor recreation opportunities abound. Frequently called 'A Sportsman's Paradise,' the area's mild climate, various lakes and rivers, and beautiful parks create the perfect setting for jogging, bicycling, water sports, hunting and fishing. For other recreational activities, Shreveport-Bossier is home to riverboat casinos and horse racing at Louisiana Downs. Additional entertainment venues include the Brookshire Grocery Arena which hosts numerous musical events, comedians, rodeos, children’s events and ice-skating productions, among others. Shreveport also hosts dozens of festivals such as Mardi Gras, Louisiana Prize Fest, the Red River Revel, and Taco Wars, with regional food and music, and offers regular theatrical productions, ballet performances, as well as performances by the Shreveport Symphony and the Shreveport Opera. Shreveport is also home to the American Rose Garden."
    },
    "https://www.lsus.edu/Documents/HR/Job%20Opportunities/2026%20Visiting%20Instructor%20of%20Mathematics.docx": {
      "title": "Visiting Instructor of Mathematics",
      "description": "Visiting Instructor of Mathematics. Full-Time Visiting Instructor Position in Mathematics\nLSU Shreveport invites applications for a full-time, 4.5-month instructor position starting in January 2027. The primary responsibilities include teaching undergraduate general education mathematics courses and co-requisite labs. We seek an energetic, dedicated colleague who is passionate about undergraduate education and skilled in active, student-centered learning methods. Excellent communication skills and a commitment to student success are essential.\nResponsibilities:\nTeach four courses including college algebra, mathematics concepts, and corresponding co-requisite labs.\nTeach in both face-to-face and online settings when required.\nConduct in-person office hours on LSUS campus.\nDemonstrate a commitment to service within the department, college, university, and community.\nRequired Qualifications:\nA master’s degree or equivalent in mathematical sciences or a closely related field.\nDemonstrated potential for excellence in college-level teaching.\nPreferred Qualifications:\nProven effectiveness using student-centered, active-learning teaching strategies and strong interpersonal communication skills.\nSalary and Benefits:\nCompetitive salary commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package, including health insurance, retirement plan options, and professional development support.\nApplication Process:\nDigital application should be sent to (with “Visiting Instructor of Mathematics” as the subject line) to mathematics@lsus.edu\nReview of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled.\nFor accessibility accommodations during any part of the hiring process, contact disabilityservices@lsus.edu / 318-797-5116.\nLSUS is an Equal Opportunity Employer.\nApplication materials must include:\nLetter of application\nCurrent curriculum vitae\nThree current letters of reference (at least one must address your teaching ability)\nTeaching statement and sample syllabus\nList of courses taught (including teaching assistant experience, if applicable)\nAbout the Department of Mathematics:\nThe Department of Mathematics delivers foundational general education courses for all undergraduates alongside advanced, specialized programs for science and math majors. Our faculty members are award-winning educators recognized university-wide for their dedication, passion, and teaching excellence. Bringing diverse expertise across pure and applied mathematics, our faculty also spearheads innovation as the coordinators of the university's new interdisciplinary Data Science program.\nAbout LSU Shreveport:\nLSUS is a teaching-focused, regional public university with a mission that balances excellence in teaching, active scholarship, and community engagement. Named by the U.S. Department of Education as one of the most affordable colleges in Louisiana, LSU Shreveport offers high-quality classroom instruction with very affordable tuition rates. In addition, LSUS offers many forms of financial aid, including scholarships, on-campus employment, and graduate assistantships. With recent expansion in the university’s infrastructure and investment in educational technology, these improvements continue to enhance the student experience at LSU Shreveport, and the University has proven itself to be an institution of opportunity and quality.\nAbout Shreveport:\nShreveport is the third-most populous city in the state of Louisiana after New Orleans and Baton Rouge, with the Shreveport-Bossier City metropolitan area home to a population of nearly 400,000. The Shreveport-Bossier City area offers an attractive quality of life, combining the conveniences of a big city with the warmth and hospitality of a smaller town. The Shreveport-Bossier City area offers a low cost of living, affordable housing, and many diverse dining and entertainment options. Exceptional outdoor recreation opportunities abound. Frequently called \"A Sportsman's Paradise,\" the area's mild climate, various lakes and rivers, and beautiful parks create the perfect setting for jogging, bicycling, water sports, hunting and fishing. For other recreational activities, Shreveport-Bossier is home to riverboat casinos and horse racing at Louisiana Downs. Additional entertainment venues include the Brookshire Grocery Arena which hosts numerous musical events, comedians, rodeos, children’s events and ice-skating productions, among others. Shreveport also hosts dozens of festivals such as Mardi Gras, Louisiana Prize Fest, the Red River Revel, and Taco Wars, with regional food and music, and offers regular theatrical productions, ballet performances, as well as performances by the Shreveport Symphony, the Shreveport Opera, and Shreveport Little Theatre. Shreveport is also home to the American Rose Garden and the R.W. Norton Art Gallery."
    },
    "https://www.lsus.edu/Documents/HR/Job%20Opportunities/2026%20Instructor%20of%20Mathematics.docx": {
      "title": "Instructor of Mathematics",
      "description": "Instructor of Mathematics. Full-Time Instructor Position in Mathematics\nLSU Shreveport invites applications for a full-time, 9-month instructor position starting in August 2027. The primary responsibilities include teaching undergraduate general education mathematics courses and co-requisite labs. We seek an energetic, dedicated colleague who is passionate about undergraduate education and skilled in active, student-centered learning methods. Excellent communication skills and a commitment to student success are essential.\nResponsibilities:\nTeach four courses including college algebra, mathematics concepts, and corresponding co-requisite labs.\nTeach in both face-to-face and online settings when required.\nConduct in-person office hours on LSUS campus.\nDemonstrate a commitment to service within the department, college, university, and community.\nRequired Qualifications:\nA master’s degree or equivalent in mathematical sciences or a closely related field.\nDemonstrated potential for excellence in college-level teaching.\nPreferred Qualifications:\nProven effectiveness using student-centered, active-learning teaching strategies and strong interpersonal communication skills.\nSalary and Benefits:\nCompetitive salary commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package, including health insurance, retirement plan options, and professional development support.\nApplication Process:\nDigital application should be sent to (with “Instructor of Mathematics” as the subject line) to mathematics@lsus.edu\nReview of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled.\nFor accessibility accommodations during any part of the hiring process, contact disabilityservices@lsus.edu / 318-797-5116.\nLSUS is an Equal Opportunity Employer.\nApplication materials must include:\nLetter of application\nCurrent curriculum vitae\nThree current letters of reference (at least one must address your teaching ability)\nTeaching statement and sample syllabus\nList of courses taught (including teaching assistant experience, if applicable)\nTranscripts (unofficial)\nAbout the Department of Mathematics:\nThe Department of Mathematics delivers foundational general education courses for all undergraduates alongside advanced, specialized programs for science and math majors. Our faculty members are award-winning educators recognized university-wide for their dedication, passion, and teaching excellence. Bringing diverse expertise across pure and applied mathematics, our faculty also spearheads innovation as the coordinators of the university's new interdisciplinary Data Science program.\nAbout LSU Shreveport:\nLSUS is a teaching-focused, regional public university with a mission that balances excellence in teaching, active scholarship, and community engagement. Named by the U.S. Department of Education as one of the most affordable colleges in Louisiana, LSU Shreveport offers high-quality classroom instruction with very affordable tuition rates. In addition, LSUS offers many forms of financial aid, including scholarships, on-campus employment, and graduate assistantships. With recent expansion in the university’s infrastructure and investment in educational technology, these improvements continue to enhance the student experience at LSU Shreveport, and the University has proven itself to be an institution of opportunity and quality.\nAbout Shreveport:\nShreveport is the third-most populous city in the state of Louisiana after New Orleans and Baton Rouge, with the Shreveport-Bossier City metropolitan area home to a population of nearly 400,000. The Shreveport-Bossier City area offers an attractive quality of life, combining the conveniences of a big city with the warmth and hospitality of a smaller town. The Shreveport-Bossier City area offers a low cost of living, affordable housing, and many diverse dining and entertainment options. Exceptional outdoor recreation opportunities abound. Frequently called \"A Sportsman's Paradise,\" the area's mild climate, various lakes and rivers, and beautiful parks create the perfect setting for jogging, bicycling, water sports, hunting and fishing. For other recreational activities, Shreveport-Bossier is home to riverboat casinos and horse racing at Louisiana Downs. Additional entertainment venues include the Brookshire Grocery Arena which hosts numerous musical events, comedians, rodeos, children’s events and ice-skating productions, among others. Shreveport also hosts dozens of festivals such as Mardi Gras, Louisiana Prize Fest, the Red River Revel, and Taco Wars, with regional food and music, and offers regular theatrical productions, ballet performances, as well as performances by the Shreveport Symphony, the Shreveport Opera, and Shreveport Little Theatre. Shreveport is also home to the American Rose Garden and the R.W. Norton Art Gallery."
    },
    "https://www.lsus.edu/Documents/HR/Job%20Opportunities/2026%20Director%20First%20Year%20Writing.docx": {
      "title": "Director of First-Year Writing",
      "description": "Director of First-Year Writing. One University Place\nShreveport, LA 71115-2399\n318-797-5369\nOne University Place\nShreveport, LA 71115-2399\n318-797-5369\nDirector of First-Year Writing\nThe Department of English and Foreign Languages at Louisiana State University Shreveport (LSUS) invites applications for a Director of First-Year Writing at the rank of Instructor starting August 2027. This is a permanent, non-tenure-track position.\nResponsibilities:\nAssume administrative and advisory duties as Director of First-Year Writing\nTeach three courses each semester, with a focus on freshman composition. Teaching is mostly face-to-face but will also include online instruction.\nChair the departmental writing committee\nWork with the Office of Student Success to align first-year composition with our first-year experience programming\nAssist in mentoring adjunct and dual-enrollment instructors\nParticipate in service activities at the university, college, and department levels, as well as in community and profession.\nParticipate in the assessment of student learning outcomes and program effectiveness.\nQualifications:\nA PhD in Rhetoric, Composition or Writing Program Administration is preferred. PhD-qualified candidates in a closely related field or MA-qualified applicants with relevant experience may also be considered.\nEvidence of effective writing teaching\nPrior administrative or supervisory experience is desirable.\nSalary and Benefits:\nSalary commensurate with experience\nComprehensive benefits package, including health insurance, retirement plan options, and professional development support\nApplication Process:\nTo apply for this position, please send a CV, cover letter, copies of graduate transcripts, and contact details of three references to Cassandra.falke@lsus.edu with the subject line “First-Year Writing Director Application.” Review of complete applications will begin on November 9, 2026. Consideration of applications submitted later is not guaranteed. Candidates selected for an interview will be asked to provide three letters of recommendation. LSUS is an Affirmative Action and Equal Opportunity Employer."
    },
    "https://www.lsus.edu/Documents/HR/Job%20Opportunities/2026%20Instructor%20-%20English.docx": {
      "title": "Instructor of English",
      "description": "Instructor of English. One University Place\nShreveport, LA 71115-2399\n318-797-5369\nOne University Place\nShreveport, LA 71115-2399\n318-797-5369\nInstructor of English\nThe Department of English and Foreign Languages at Louisiana State University Shreveport (LSUS) invites applications for a one-year full-time appointment at the rank of Instructor starting August 2027. Full-time instructor appointments may be renewed annually upon satisfactory performance reviews.\nResponsibilities:\nTeach four courses each semester, including freshman composition and general education literature surveys. Teaching is mostly face-to-face but will also include online instruction.\nTeach upper-division classes as needed based on qualifications, and experience.\nParticipate in service activities (such as committee work) at the university, college, and department levels, as well as in community and profession.\nParticipate in the assessment of student learning outcomes and program effectiveness.\nQualifications:\nA master’s degree with at least 18 graduate hours in English is required. A PhD in English is preferred.\nEvidence of effective teaching\nSalary and Benefits:\nSalary commensurate with experience\nComprehensive benefits package, including health insurance, retirement plan options, and professional development support\nApplication Process:\nTo apply for this position, a CV, cover letter, copies of graduate transcripts, and contact details of three references should be sent electronically to Cassandra.falke@lsus.edu with the Subject line “English Instructor Application.” Review of complete applications will begin on November 9, 2026. Consideration of application submitted later is not guaranteed. Candidates selected for the interview will be asked to provide three letters of recommendation. LSUS is an Affirmative Action and Equal Opportunity Employer."
    },
    "https://www.governmentjobs.com/careers/louisiana/jobs/5497119/assistant-director-practical-nursing-program": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Practical Nursing Program",
      "description": "Assistant Director, Practical Nursing Program Salary Depends on Qualifications Location Shreveport, LA Job Type Unclassified Job Number 226647 Department LSU-Shreveport Opening Date 09/28/2026 Closing Date 10/31/2026 11:59 PM Central Description Benefits About this Job LSU in Shreveport invites applications for the position of Assistant Director of the Licensed Practical Nursing Program (LPN). The Assistant Director supports the Program Director in the administration, coordination, and continuous improvement of the LPN program. The Assistant Director provides leadership in day-to-day program operations, supports faculty and students, coordinates clinical education activities, and helps ensure that program requirements, institutional policies, and applicable state and accreditation standards are met. The Assistant Director works collaboratively with the Program Director, nursing faculty, clinical partners, college administration, and student support personnel to promote student success and maintain a high-quality practical nursing program. The position may include teaching responsibilities in the classroom, laboratory, simulation, and/or clinical settings based on program needs. This is a 12-month on campus position based in Shreveport, Louisiana. Minimum Qualifications Required Qualifications: Qualifications from Louisiana State Board of Practical Nurse Examiners (LSBPNE): · Hold a current, valid license to practice as a registered nurse (RN) in Louisiana. · Meet the approval of the LSBPNE prior to teaching. · Minimum of four years of experience in medical-surgical nursing or nursing education. o At least one of those four years must have been as a medical-surgical hospital staff nurse providing direct patient care. · Must have practiced as a nurse for a minimum of six full-time months during the three years immediately preceding application. Job Duties and Other Information Responsibilities and Requirements: · Proficiency with computer applications such as Microsoft Office Suite, Canva, etc. · Supports program director with day-to-day operation of the nursing program to include but not limited to: Telephone communication with the public concerning matters of the nursing program, preparing material for orientation of students and faculty, supporting an electronic and hard copy filing system, assisting with clinical placement of students, and dealing with student issues and problems. · Evaluate program, course, and student learning outcomes and adjust to ensure students receive what they need to succeed. · Orient and assist new nursing faculty with classroom instruction techniques, clinical information and guidelines, computer and software usage including but not limited to: Moodle, ATI, The Point, ExamSoft. · Counsel and advise students regarding grades and clinical/classroom issues and work with them to develop a plan to achieve success. · Work directly with the LSBPNE to ensure students are ready and capable of testing for the National Council Licensure Examination (NCLEX) when they graduate. · Teach in the Nursing Program by employing a variety of innovative and instructional techniques that will enhance student learning. · Ensure Lesson Plan Books and other paperwork are up to date according to LSPBNE standards. · Works closely with the program director and has the knowledge and skillset to transition into that role when the director is out for meetings or other responsibilities. How to Apply: No Civil Service test score is required in order to be considered for this vacancy. To apply for this vacancy, click on the “Apply” link above and complete an electronic application, which can be used for this vacancy as well as future job opportunities. Applicants are responsible for checking the status of their application to determine where they are in the recruitment process. Further status message information is located under the Information section of the Current Job Opportunities page. *Resumes WILL NOT be accepted in lieu of completed education and experience sections on your application. Applications may be rejected if incomplete.* For additional information concerning this job posting contact: Jennifer Isaac LSUS Department of Human Resource Management Room 108 Administration Building, LSUS (318) 797-5279 LSUS is an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer and encourages applications from women and minorities. For more information about LSU Shreveport go to www.lsus.edu LSUS is a State As a Model (SAME) employer that promotes affirmative strategies and goals for employment of individuals with disabilities. Benefits for unclassified employees are determined by the individual hiring authority. Employer State of Louisiana Address 1201 N 3rd St Baton Rouge, Louisiana, 70802 Phone (866) 783-5462 Website http://agency.governmentjobs.com/louisiana/default.cfm Apply"
    },
    "https://www.governmentjobs.com/careers/louisiana/jobs/5501542/director-of-early-college": {
      "title": "Director of Early College",
      "description": "Director of Early College Salary Depends on Qualifications Location Shreveport, LA Job Type Unclassified Job Number 226808 Department LSU-Shreveport Opening Date 10/01/2026 Closing Date 10/31/2026 11:59 PM Central Description Benefits About this Job The Director of Early College at LSUS serves as a strategic and academic leader responsible for driving the expansion, implementation, and academic quality of the university’s rapidly growing Dual Enrollment (DE) and Academic Excellence (AEP) programs. The Director acts as the primary university liaison for external partner school districts, ensures strict academic quality and accreditation compliance (SACS and NACEP), and serves as the lead administrative developer for new high school initiatives. The Director manages operational, instructional, and administrative processes, collaborating closely with Admissions, Accounting, Records, and academic departments to streamline workflows and support student retention. This position reports directly to the Associate Dean of Arts and Sciences. Travel to regional partner schools is required to facilitate site visits, advisory sessions, and professional development. The successful candidate might occasionally be invited to teach a course in their discipline. This summary is not intended to be a comprehensive or all-inclusive description of duties and responsibilities. Minimum Qualifications Required Qualifications: • Bachelor’s degree from a regionally accredited college or university. • Three years of experience in Early College administration or teaching, educational administration, K-12 relations, program coordination, or academic affairs. • Ability to collaborate across academic and administrative units to achieve institutional goals. • Proficient with Microsoft Office Suite. • Valid driver’s license and ability to travel to Caddo/Bossier schools. Preferred Qualifications: • Master’s degree from a regionally accredited college or university. • Experience managing Dual Enrollment or Early College programs. • Knowledge of higher education accreditation, compliance, and quality assurance processes. • Ability to work both independently and effectively with multiple stakeholders. Job Specification Core Competencies: • Communication: Ability to adjust communication style to suit the audience/customer/situation. • Collaboration: Ability to work effectively in group settings. • Service to Customer and LSUS: Ability to focus on providing positive experiences for stakeholders. • Delivering Results: Ability to focus on achieving outcomes. • Problem-Solving: Ability to overcome barriers to success. • Leading Others: Ability to inspire, guide, and support individuals and teams towards achieving common goals. Physical and Environmental Demands Light Work - Exerting up to 20 pounds of force occasionally, and/or up to 10 pounds of force frequently, and/or a negligible amount of force constantly (Constantly: activity or condition exists 2/3 or more of the time) to move objects. Physical demand requirements are in excess of those for Sedentary Work. Even though the weight lifted may be only a negligible amount, a job should be rated Light Work when it requires walking or standing to a significant degree or when it requires sitting most of the time but entails pushing and/or pulling even though the weight of those materials is negligible. Job Duties and Other Information Duties & Responsibilities: Academic Quality, Compliance, and Onboarding · Ensure all high school Dual Enrollment and Early College programs comply with SACS and NACEP accreditation standards. · Manage new teacher credentialing, vetting, and academic onboarding, ensuring that all off-site instructors meet necessary accreditation requirements. · Assign dedicated faculty liaisons to Dual Enrollment teachers and assist in providing discipline-specific training and onboarding materials before the academic year begins. · Develop professional development opportunities for faculty liaisons, executing training programs at least twice per academic year. · Coordinate site visits and manage approval processes for new partner schools. · Collaborate with the Associate Dean of Arts and Sciences on curriculum alignment and academic policy enforcement. Program Administration, Operations, and Workflow · Establish and continuously optimize workflows for all Early College processes, coordinating directly with Admissions, Accounting, and the Registrar’s Office to streamline cross-departmental communication. · Review and update the Early College Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) annually to ensure operational adherence across all partnerships. · Coordinate and execute major program events, including Dual Enrollment Professional Development and Dual Enrollment Day. · Oversee registration, course planning, and student tracking metrics, ensuring accurate enrollment reporting and program data compilation. · Manage financial administrative processes, including payroll processing for Dual Enrollment teachers and prompt compensation processing for faculty liaisons. · Process official student paperwork, including student course withdrawals, following appropriate registrar guidelines. K-12 Partnerships, Advising, and Student Engagement · Build and nurture strong institutional partnerships with regional high schools, districts, and school systems. · Travel to partner high schools to meet with, present to, and advise prospective Academic Excellence Program (AEP) students. · Coordinate with partner schools to ensure the prompt, accurate, and secure submission of student registration materials to the Admissions Office. · Represent LSUS at Board of Regents Dual Enrollment workshops and NACEP annual conferences to maintain awareness of regional/national trends. · Act as the primary point of contact for high school administrators, counselors, parents, and students navigating the college-transition pipeline. Other special projects, duties and responsibilities as assigned. How to Apply: No Civil Service test score is required in order to be considered for this vacancy. To apply for this vacancy, click on the “Apply” link above and complete an electronic application, which can be used for this vacancy as well as future job opportunities. Applicants are responsible for checking the status of their application to determine where they are in the recruitment process. Further status message information is located under the Information section of the Current Job Opportunities page. *Resumes WILL NOT be accepted in lieu of completed education and experience sections on your application. Applications may be rejected if incomplete.* For additional information concerning this job posting contact: Jennifer Isaac LSUS Department of Human Resource Management Room 108 Administration Building, LSUS (318) 797-5279 LSUS is an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer and encourages applications from women and minorities. For more information about LSU Shreveport go to www.lsus.edu LSUS is a State As a Model (SAME) employer that promotes affirmative strategies and goals for employment of individuals with disabilities. Benefits for unclassified employees are determined by the individual hiring authority. Employer State of Louisiana Address 1201 N 3rd St Baton Rouge, Louisiana, 70802 Phone (866) 783-5462 Website http://agency.governmentjobs.com/louisiana/default.cfm Apply"
    },
    "https://www.governmentjobs.com/careers/louisiana/jobs/5507271/operating-engineer-2": {
      "title": "Operating Engineer 2",
      "description": "Operating Engineer 2 Salary $3,410.00 - $6,136.00 Monthly Location Shreveport, LA Job Type Classified Job Number 227004 Department LSU-Shreveport Opening Date 10/07/2026 Closing Date 10/20/2026 11:59 PM Central Description Benefits About this Job The Operating Engineer II serves as the hands-on expert behind the operation and maintenance of boilers, HVAC, refrigeration, and utility systems that support daily facility operations. Through preventive maintenance, equipment monitoring, system adjustments, and mechanical troubleshooting, this position helps ensure safe, reliable, and energy-efficient building environments. Success in this role requires technical skill, attention to detail, and a commitment to keeping critical infrastructure operating at peak performance. Minimum Qualifications Two years of experience or training in the operation or maintenance of large stationary engines or mechanical equipment. NECESSARY SPECIAL REQUIREMENT: For those positions in which incumbents will be working with and handling refrigerants, possession of an appropriate EPA refrigerant recycling and recovery certification for technicians will be required (as required under Section 608 of the Clean Air Act, 1990). Job Specification The official job specifications for this role, as defined by the State Civil Service, can be found here . Job Duties and Other Information Job Duties: Performs preventative maintenance on boilers and related equipment such as ventilating and refrigeration equipment, air conditioning machinery, generators, suction fans, pumps and compressors. Maintains logbooks detailing operations pertaining to boilers and or pumping equipment Performs minor repairs on boilers and related equipment Starts and stops pumps as needed; fires gas or oil fired boilers; adjusts airflow Regulates water level and reads gauges to check water level, steam pressure and operation of valves and auxiliary pumps Position-Specific Details: Appointment Type: Probational or Promotional How to Apply: No Civil Service test score is required in order to be considered for this vacancy. To apply for this vacancy, click on the “Apply” link above and complete an electronic application, which can be used for this vacancy as well as future job opportunities. Applicants are responsible for checking the status of their application to determine where they are in the recruitment process. Further status message information is located under the Information section of the Current Job Opportunities page. * Information to support your eligibility for the position must be included in the application (i.e., relevant, detailed experience/education). Resumes WILL NOT be accepted in lieu of completed education and experience sections on your application. Applications may be rejected if incomplete. Contact Information: For additional information concerning this job posting contact: Jennifer Isaac LSUS Department of Human Resource Management Room 108 Administration Building, LSUS (318) 797-5279 LSUS is an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer and encourages applications from women and minorities. For more information about LSU Shreveport go to www.lsus.edu LSUS is a State As a Model (SAME) employer that promotes affirmative strategies and goals for employment of individuals with disabilities. Louisiana State Government represents a wide variety of career options and offers an outstanding opportunity to make a difference through public service. With an array of career opportunities in every major metropolitan center and in many rural areas, state employment provides an outstanding option to begin or continue your career. As a state employee, you will earn competitive pay, choose from a variety of benefits, and have access to a great professional development program: Insurance Coverage More information can be found at https://info.groupbenefits.org/ Parental Leave – Up to six weeks paid parental leave More information can be found at https://www.civilservice.louisiana.gov/Parental-Leave/Default.aspx Holidays and Leave – State employees receive the following paid holidays each year: New Year's Day Martin Luther King, Jr. Day, Mardi Gras, Good Friday, Memorial Day, Independence Day, Labor Day, Veteran's Day, Thanksgiving Day and Christmas Day. ***** Additional holidays may be proclaimed by the Governor State employees earn sick and annual leave which can be accumulated and saved for future use. Your accrual rate increases as your years of service increase. Retirement – State of Louisiana employees are eligible to participate in various retirement systems (based on the type of appointment and agency for which an employee works). These retirement systems provide retirement allowances and other benefits for state officers and employees and their beneficiaries. State retirement systems may include (but are not limited to): Louisiana State Employees Retirement System ( www.lasersonline.org ). LASERS has provided this video to give you more detailed information about their system Teacher's Retirement System of Louisiana ( www.trsl.org ), Louisiana School Employees Retirement System ( www.lsers.net ), among others Employer State of Louisiana Address 1201 N 3rd St Baton Rouge, Louisiana, 70802 Phone (866) 783-5462 Website http://agency.governmentjobs.com/louisiana/default.cfm Apply"
    }
  },
